From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:12:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 041/342] libroot, kernel: arm64 memcpy, memmove and memset
 from Arm Optimized Routines

The generic C memcpy copies byte by byte when source and destination are
not aligned alike. These are Arm's optimized-routines v26.07
(MIT OR Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception), unmodified: memcpy-advsimd.S
(which also provides memmove), memcpy.S, memset.S, memset-scalar.S and
asmdefs.h. Small MIT wrappers give them Haiku's names:

- libroot (arch_memcpy.S, arch_memset.S): the Advanced SIMD memcpy and
  memmove, and memset. musl's C memmove is left out on arm64, also in
  the runtime_loader.
- kernel (kernel_memcpy.S, kernel_memset.S): the scalar versions, which
  need no FPU state; the kernel keeps its own C memmove.

On the ODROID-C2 (MB/s, aligned / misaligned): memcpy 64 B 1637 -> 3637,
1 KiB 3632 -> 6345, 16 KiB 4041 -> 6764 / 499 -> 5398; memset 1 KiB
5194 -> 12895, 16 KiB 6060 -> 14147. Copies of 4 MiB and more are bound
by memory either way. Both Cortex-A53 erratum scans are clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
